 u69 u63 u69 u74 u20 u75 u6e u73 u75 u70 u70 u6f u72 u74 u65 u64 u2d u6c u65 u67 u61 u63 u79 u2d u74 u79 u70 u65 u20 u65 u72 u72 u6f u72 u20 u72 u61 u74 u68 u65 u72 u20 u74 u68 u61 u6e u20 u69 u6e u74 u65 u72 u70 u72 u65 u74 u69 u6e u67 u20 u6f u72 u20 u63 u6f u6e u76 u65 u72 u74 u69 u6e u67 u20 u69 u74 u73 u20 u61 u6d u62 u69 u67 u75 u6f u75 u73 u20 u70 u61 u79 u6c u6f u61 u64 u2e u20 u57 u72 u69 u74 u65 u72 u73 u20 u6d u75 u73 u74 u20 u6e u6f u74 u20 u65 u6d u69 u74 u20 u74 u79 u70 u65 u73 u20 u31 u20 u6f u72 u20 u34 u2e u20 u54 u79 u70 u65 u20 u62 u79 u74 u65 u73 u20 u35 u2e u2e u32 u35 u35 u20 u61 u72 u65 u20 u72 u65 u73 u65 u72 u76 u65 u64 u2e # "Like DcUtf, but begins with magic DcMM\\0 followed by UUID d5ab819e-9b8e-4d61-adbc-933e8851e79a as 16 binary bytes. Every top-level Dc 203 is followed by a type byte and a 128-bit big-endian payload-byte length, including zero-length payloads. Type 2 then carries a 128-bit big-endian metadata-byte length, followed by that many DcUtf bytes encoding [type]? [checksum]?, then the raw binary payload. The checksum covers the raw payload bytes; its hash type is declared in the checksum's own Dc 203 frame. The metadata block may be empty. The terminating Dc 204 is implicit. Writers of binary and typed-checksum encapsulations use type 2; textual Dc 203 writers emit base64 payload Dcs with the decoded payload-byte length and preserve declared types and checksums. DcUtf payload bytes can use type 2 with a declared DcUtf type; they are not spliced into the surrounding character stream. Legacy type 0 stores raw binary and type 3 adds a 32-byte binary SHA-256 checksum following the payload length; both remain readable. Types 1 and 4 are retired: their original intent was, I believe, to indicate the encoding of the stored data (which it is to be interpreted as), but the original implementation used them to indicate the encoding in which the data was stored (and emitted non-encapsulation Dcs inside Dc 203, which would be invalid syntax). The use cases for them can be met by using type byte 2 with type metadata. As type bytes 1 and 4 were never used, readers reject either byte with an explicit unsupported-legacy-type error rather than interpreting or converting its ambiguous payload. Writers must not emit types 1 or 4. Type bytes 5..255 are reserved."
